  "controlEntitlements": {
    "$note": "Which licence actually unlocks each Secure Score control, so a control's spend derives from the SKU that entitles it rather than from a share of a tenant-wide pot. Values are service plan names as Graph reports them in subscribedSkus.servicePlans; a control is entitled when the tenant owns ANY listed plan. An empty array means no paid licence is required -- that control draws no spend. A control absent from this map falls back to serviceDefaults below. EXCHANGE_S_FOUNDATION is deliberately excluded: it is a stub plan present in almost every SKU including Visio and Project, grants no mailbox, and treating it as Exchange Online made unrelated products appear to fund email security.",
    "$research": "Tier boundaries verified against Microsoft Learn, September 2026. Defender for Office: the EOP/Defender split follows the section boundary in 'Recommendations for Microsoft 365 security settings' -- anti-malware, anti-spam and spoof intelligence are EOP; impersonation protection, mailbox intelligence, phishing thresholds, Safe Links and Safe Attachments are Defender for Office 365. Entra: risk policies and leaked-credential detection require P2 per the ID Protection licensing table; the custom banned password list requires P1 while the global list is Free per the password protection licensing table; cloud-only SSPR requires Business Standard or above, or P1. Superset plans are listed alongside the minimum, so THREAT_INTELLIGENCE satisfies a Defender for Office P1 requirement and AAD_PREMIUM_P2 satisfies a P1 one. Deployment, not just entitlement: a licence can make a control SCORED while a different one is needed to DEPLOY it, and the cost of closing a gap is the latter. Microsoft's ASR deployment guide names Intune endpoint security policy as the recommended method and states it requires Intune Plan 1, so the Defender for Endpoint device controls carry Intune rather than Defender. Group Policy and PowerShell remain licence-free alternatives for ASR, so this is the supported-path cost rather than the only possible one. Correction from an earlier pass: aad_password_protection covers on-premises AD DS, which requires P1 -- only the cloud global banned password list is Free.",
