    "$layer": "A Secure Score control can have three different licences attached and they are not the same one. For an ASR rule: the CAPABILITY is Microsoft Defender Antivirus, free in Windows; the VISIBILITY -- why the control is scored for this tenant at all -- is Defender for Endpoint Plan 2; the DEPLOYMENT path Microsoft recommends is Intune Plan 1, though Group Policy and PowerShell do it for free. This map encodes VISIBILITY: the licence that makes the control assessable for this tenant. It is the only layer where the licence is genuinely tenant-specific, it is money actually committed, and it makes the report answer what the security spend is earning. Capability would drop 113 of 118 device controls to 'no licence needed'; deployment would charge for a licence a customer may legitimately not need. See docs/CONTROL-ENTITLEMENTS.md.",
    "nonUserLicensing": {
      "$note": "Some SKUs license identities that are not the tenant's people -- AI agents, bots, sandboxes -- and their service plans must not be read as entitling a security control for human users. Microsoft Agent 365 Frontier is the case that exposed this: it carries an E5-grade plan list including AAD_PREMIUM_P2, MIP_S_CLP2 and ADALLOM_S_STANDALONE, so on plan names alone it looked like the licence entitling 60 tenant controls. It entitles Agent 365 functionality. Graph offers no signal to separate them: appliesTo reads 'User' on every plan in that SKU, including the ones named FOR_AGENTS. Detected by marker plans so future agent SKUs are covered without an edit, with an explicit list for anything the markers miss.",
      "markerServicePlans": [
        "AGENT_365",
        "AGENT_365_TOOLS"
      ],
      "markerServicePlanPatterns": [
        "_FOR_AGENTS$",
        "_FOR_ASSISTIVE_AGENTS$"
      ],
      "skuPartNumbers": []
    }
  }
}
